+- **Code-hygiene trio + the `hound`.** Three orthogonal cleanup scopes: **absorption** (cross-product file-level — `noctus.seed.report`), **fusion** (cross-tool function-level — `noctus.seed.scan_fusions`), **optimization** (intra-file line-level — `noctus.seed.scan_optimizations`). **`noctus.hound.scan` is the single entry point** — runs all three ∧ emits a `next_action` priority recommendation. Default for *"what cleanup is most urgent?"*. Keeper-analog: keeper = regulatory (compliance contracts); hound = curatorial (hygiene candidates). Calibration: structural-duplicate filter in `scan_repetition` (default-on) skips empty `__init__.py` markers + already-absorbed re-export shims — without it the absorption queue is dominated by false positives. → `KB § PATTERNS/seed-absorption.md § noctus.hound.scan` + `KB § 06-AGENTS.md § Code-hygiene trio`
+- **Storage-hygiene trio + the `mole`.** Third member of the regulatory/curatorial/custodial trio: keeper guards laws, hound sniffs hygiene, **mole burrows for storage waste**. Three orthogonal scopes: **artifacts** (regenerable caches/builds — `__pycache__`/`.pytest_cache`/`dist`), **environments** (venv/node_modules duplication — *advisory-only*; pnpm-workspace candidates), **worktrees** (stale `.claude/worktrees/agent-*/`). Single entry point: `bash scripts/mole.sh scan` → emits `next_action`; `bash scripts/mole.sh sweep --force` is the destructive variant (dry-run default). **Active triggers**: pre-dispatch (orchestrator) + pre-commit (bloat warning) + bootstrap (cleanup-before-hydrate). Safety: never deletes uncommitted work, main worktree, sibling workspaces, unmerged branches, `.env`, ∨ migration files. Originated 2026-05-11 from `mapfile`-bash3-silent-no-op — methodology scripts MUST be bash 3.x compatible. → `KB § PATTERNS/storage-hygiene.md`
+- **Triage at decision time — `[F]` / `[R]` / `[A]`.** Every divergence from ideals lands on one of three explicit outcomes: **`[F]` formalize** (extend framework/seed), **`[R]` refactor** (align with contract), ∨ **`[A]` accept-with-rationale** (catalog in `KB § PATTERNS/accept-with-rationale.md` — durable register that survives project folder deletion). "Accept" is a real landing — paperwork keeps it from going silent. Recurrence flips prior `[A]` outcomes toward `[F]`. → `KB § 01-PHILOSOPHY.md § Triage at decision time` + `KB § PATTERNS/accept-with-rationale.md`
+- **Safety nets capture failures; failures become learnings; methodology evolves.** Methodology is incomplete by design. Safety nets — `git merge` for the merging gap, drive-by-exception for hook false-positives, `git stash` for switch blockers — keep the system working when methodology hits a gap. **Capture the learning** (`KB § PATTERNS/project-execution.md § 2.11 Phase enrichment loop`), **update the methodology** (three-way sync), **gap closes structurally** so future occurrences hit the updated rule, ¬ the gap. The safety net activating IS the methodology working — it caught what the rule didn't yet cover. Anti-patterns: bypassing the safety net to avoid surfacing the gap (`--force` push instead of merging); capturing the failure but skipping the methodology update (lives only in §11 prose); repeating the same catch without formalizing (recurrence rule fires but methodology never evolves). → `KB § 01-PHILOSOPHY.md § Safety nets capture failures; failures become learnings; methodology evolves`
+- **Branching-first orchestration — parallelize by default; orchestration STAYS with the orchestrator. Architect plans + dispatches + stays-with-user; engineers build.** **Orchestrator ≡ architect** — plans + dispatches + evaluates + stays available for user-facing ideation while engineers (subagents) do heavy lifting in isolated worktrees. **Subagents ARE engineers** (single ∨ team, per parallelism opportunity) — focused briefs, narrow context, build the chunks the architect defined, report back. Architect's default mental model on incoming work: "can this be chunked into parallel branches?" If yes — `git worktree add` per engineer (`KB § PATTERNS/branching-and-merging.md § 16`); dispatch in a single `Task` tool-use turn (true parallelism); maintain findings.md aggregating slips / errors / lessons / surprises (§ 17). If no — serial, ∨ master-tree-parallel-batches for N≥2 same-shape children. **Engineers EXECUTE focused chunks; they never PLAN orchestration** — architect's broad-context + session-spanning conversation IS the planning value. **Engineer findings are evaluated locally + applied immediately when applicable** — methodology gaps with clear fixes get amended SAME SESSION (KB ↔ CLAUDE ↔ memory three-way sync); small in-scope cleanups go inline; cross-product follow-ups file future projects; routine completions bundle in retrospective. Deferring an applicable fix = silent-error shape; immediate implementation is the rule. **Serial requires justification** (logged as a learning). Anti-patterns: serial-by-default when parallel was feasible; engineers in separate messages (= serial); forcing parallel when chunks depend; skipping chunk identification; **delegating orchestration itself to an engineer**; **dispatching N engineers into the same worktree** (race-prone); **deferring fixes applicable now** (debt). Conversational dimension: architect stays available for the user while engineers work — the user thinks-with the architect, ¬ engineers. → `KB § 01-PHILOSOPHY.md § Branching-first orchestration` + `§ Roles: Architect + Engineers` + `§ Knowledge tracking — durable findings file`
+- **Knowledge tracking — durable findings file for any non-trivial work.** Any non-trivial project / feature / orchestration maintains `findings.md` at its root capturing slips / errors / mistakes / lessons / interesting findings / discovered knowledge. Five standard categories. Append in-the-moment for surprises (freshness matters); synthesize at close into a curated knowledge artifact. Default-on for projects + master-trees + parallel-orchestration; optional for trivial direct fixes (silent skip ¬allowed — log absence to `phase_learnings` SQLite). Distinct from `phase_learnings.db` (atomic per-phase) + `live-patterns-log.md` (master-tree per-batch raw) + §11 Change log (what-we-did): findings.md is what-we-LEARNED, curated. **Architect dispatch briefs MUST include explicit Write-authorization for `findings.md` / `PROJECT.md` / proposal `.md`** — engineer subagents inherit the "*NEVER create *.md files*" default ∧ may refuse without explicit override (template at KB §17.6). → `KB § 01-PHILOSOPHY.md § Knowledge tracking` + `KB § PATTERNS/branching-and-merging.md § 17.6`
+- **Wave-based dispatch + pause-on-dependency + scoped-team economics.** Group chunks by dependency-depth: Wave N+1 dispatches only after every Wave N chunk **FF-merges** (¬ just engineer-reports). When an engineer surfaces a gap mid-flight (missing helper / unmade decision / absent primitive), **pause** the chunk, **dispatch a focused dependency team**, **resume** on merge — engineers never absorb dependencies into their own scope. Split rather than combine: a 200-LoC focused brief beats a 2000-LoC sprawling brief, even at higher token cost. **Token cost is acceptable; quality is the constraint.** When in doubt about combining, split. → `KB § PATTERNS/branching-and-merging.md § 18`
+- **No silent errors — always explicit fix opportunities.** No `except: pass`, no silent degraded fallbacks, no deferred items without a named destination, no "verification ✅" when the tail showed red. Ambiguity is a silent error — ask. Absence of findings is a claim — quote the command that confirms it. → `KB § 01-PHILOSOPHY.md § No silent errors`
+- **Three-way sync — KB, CLAUDE.md (or topical CLAUDE/<topic>.md), and memory move together.** Any rule/methodology/behavior change lives in **all three layers simultaneously**. **NEW rule ordering**: KB-first, then CLAUDE.md (or topical) pointer, then memory entry + MEMORY.md index line. **Amending an existing rule**: all three layers same session. `verify-kb-sync.sh` catches dangling KB ↔ CLAUDE.md pointers but not missing memory entries — that's the agent's discipline. → `KB § 01-PHILOSOPHY.md § Docs stay in sync`
+- **Doc-code coherence — updating a tool means updating its docs in the SAME change.** Three-way-sync covers KB ↔ CLAUDE.md ↔ memory; this rule extends it to **tool-code ↔ doc-prose**. Whenever a coding tool (script / MCP tool / keeper detector / CLI command) changes behavior — new flag, new mode, renamed detector, different severity — every doc referencing it MUST update in the **same commit**: KB pattern docs, Situation→Tool maps, CLAUDE.md routing pointers describing behavior, INDEX.md scope descriptions, inline `--help` text, README/MASTER-PROMPT references in product folders. "I'll update the doc later" ¬allowed. Discovery: `grep -rn "<tool-name>" KNOWLEDGE-BASE/ CLAUDE.md CLAUDE/ projects/ products/*/README.md`. Pre-commit hook doesn't yet enforce — agent-discipline at s3, with codification candidates (`check_doc_tool_reference_drift`, `check_mcp_tool_argument_drift`) tracked in the rule's memory entry. → `KB § PATTERNS/methodology-codification-pipeline.md § 8 (Situation → tool map)` (the most-stale-prone doc surface)
+- **Finish the session — verify, don't assume.** End-of-session checklist: `cd products/<touched>/frontend && npx vite build`; `cd products/<touched>/backend && pytest`; `cd mcp/noctusai && pytest tests/` if MCP-toolkit changed; report any regression. Don't mark "done" while a build ∨ test is red. *Every in-session change must land on green.*
+- **Never auto-commit ∨ push, except project gates; commit + push only your own work.** Two carve-outs: (a) end-of-phase local commit (no push); (b) final commit + `git push` at project close (literal last step, after folder deletion). `git status` first; never `git add .` / `-A`. Stage only files YOU authored this session — explicit-path `git add` does NOT validate authorship. Verify via `git diff --cached --name-only` before commit, `git log origin/<branch>..HEAD` before push; surprise entries → unstage / ask. → `CLAUDE/projects.md § Commit per phase, push at project close` + `KB § PATTERNS/project-execution.md § 2.10`
+- **Context budget discipline.** `CLAUDE.md` (this file) = router. `CLAUDE/<topic>.md` = topical rules, read on-demand by agent discipline (see §3). `KNOWLEDGE-BASE/` = depth. **MCP keep-list**: `noctusai` + `supabase` only — anything else needs explicit user approval. **Skills keep-list**: `update-config` / `loop` / `schedule` / `security-review` only. New rule → KB-first → CLAUDE.md (or topical) pointer → memory. New §1 bullet >80 words ⇒ trim ∧ push depth to KB. → `KB § 01-PHILOSOPHY.md § Context budget discipline`
+- **Templates cannot modify noc.** A template workspace is a sibling of noc that consumes 8 noc surfaces (CLAUDE.md, CLAUDE/, KNOWLEDGE-BASE/, .claude/, mcp/, seed/, noctusai_lib/, templates/) via read-only symlinks. Three-layer defense: (1) PRIMARY — template's pre-commit hook refuses commits touching symlinked paths ∧ non-sandbox additions without a `.promotions/<slug>.md` entry, (2) AGENT — this rule + KB depth + memory entry, (3) SYMBOLIC — chmod -h a-w on symlinks (no-op on macOS; symbolic only). Edits to symlinked surfaces belong in noc; additions destined for noc go through promotion manifest + `noctusai_promote_from_template`. → `KB § PATTERNS/template-workspace.md`
+- **Seed workspaces inherit noc whole, ¬ trimmed.** Sibling workspaces consume all 8 noc surfaces via read-only symlinks; *trim none*. Auto-load is already minimal by design (`CLAUDE.md` = router; KB depth loads on-demand — unread = uncost; `MEMORY.md` = index). Trimming breaks **seed-first analysis** (needs cross-product visibility to spot absorption candidates), **recurrence-rule scans** (need every product in tree to detect N≥2/N≥3), **KB pointer integrity** (cross-refs flagged by `verify-kb-sync.sh`), ∧ **three-way sync**. Per-product focus belongs in `products/<slug>/MASTER-PROMPT.md`, ¬ pruning. Workspace ≡ `noc + product`, ¬ `subset(noc) + product`. → `KB § PATTERNS/seed-workspace.md § Why the inherited surface is not trimmed`
+- **Parallel-agent collision protocol — STOP, wait, continue.** When a shared-file edit is reverted by another agent's work ∧ re-applying would loop, ¬ loop-fight. STOP after the second revert. **¬ file a collision-report project** — wait for the parallel agent to finish. Continue with non-colliding deliverables; catalog the deferred work in `accept-with-rationale.md` so the design intent survives the wait. Surface in end-of-work summary (name the seam + parallel project + catalog entry's short-title). Re-apply when the user signals ∨ the parallel project closes (one apply + verify no further reverts ⇒ flip catalog entry to FORMALIZED). N=2 collisions on different files in same session ⇒ pause project, surface to user. → `KB § PATTERNS/project-execution.md § 2.9`
 
